>> title: New England Boiled Dinner
>> category: Stews & Braises
>> tags: beef, corned beef, cabbage, one-pot, simmer, dinner
>> counters: One Pot
>> aka: boiled dinner, corned beef and cabbage, Yankee boiled dinner, corned beef dinner
>> servings: 6
>> time: 4 hr
>> step.1: One pot, and the whole discipline is the order things go into it. The beef takes three hours, the roots twenty minutes and the cabbage fifteen, so they go in backwards from the end and everything is done at once. Put them all in together and you get grey cabbage and hard carrots.
>> step.2: simmer 3 hr, barely moving
>> step.3: simmer the roots 20 min
>> step.4: simmer the cabbage 15 min
>> step.5: rest 15 min, carve across the grain
>> step.6: Keep the pot at a shiver, never a boil. A rolling boil squeezes brisket into string, and the same liquid that turned tough at a boil would have gone tender ten degrees lower. Ladle a little of it over the sliced meat, and keep the rest for soup.

One pot, and the whole discipline is the order things go into it. The beef takes three hours, the roots twenty minutes and the cabbage fifteen, so they go in backwards from the end and everything is done at once. Put them all in together and you get grey cabbage and hard carrots.

Simmer @corned beef brisket{4%lb}(1.8 kg; rinsed) in a #stockpot{} for ~simmer{3%hr} with @water{4%qt}(3.8 L; to cover), @bay leaves{2}, @black peppercorns{1%tsp}, @yellow onion{1%large}(halved).

Add @Yukon Gold potatoes{2%lb}(900 g; halved), @carrots{6%medium}(cut in 2-in lengths), @turnip{1%medium}(peeled and wedged) to @&(~1)pot{} and ~simmer{20%min}.

Add @green cabbage{1%small}(cut in 6 wedges through the core) to @&(~1)pot{} and ~simmer{15%min}, until a knife goes through the stem.

Lift @&(~1)boiled dinner{} out with @flat-leaf parsley{1/4%cup}(chopped), @grainy mustard{2%Tbs}, and ~rest{15%min} before carving the brisket across the grain.

Keep the pot at a shiver, never a boil. A rolling boil squeezes brisket into string, and the same liquid that turned tough at a boil would have gone tender ten degrees lower. Ladle a little of it over the sliced meat, and keep the rest for soup.
